<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Balearic Islanders</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 BCE – 123 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The tower-building peoples of Mallorca and Menorca, whose stone talaiots and taulas still dot the islands and whose slingers fought for Carthage and Rome.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>From around 1000 BCE the islanders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Mallorca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Menorca</a> developed what archaeologists call the Talaiotic culture, named after the talaiots, massive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dry-stone-construction">dry-stone</a> towers that dominated fortified villages. It grew out of the earlier Naviform culture of boat-shaped houses and stone tombs such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naveta-des-tudons">Naveta des Tudons</a> on Menorca.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>There was no single state: the islands were divided among village communities, probably led by local chiefs, living from herding, farming and some trade with Phoenician and Punic merchants, especially from nearby <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ebusus">Ebusus</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Menorca's</a> T-shaped taulas stood in sanctuary enclosures. The islanders were famed throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> as slingers and served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> in Carthaginian armies, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal's</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Accused of sheltering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>, the islands were conquered in 123 BCE by the Roman general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-balearicus">Quintus Caecilius Metellus</a>, who took the name Balearicus and founded the towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palma">Palma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pollentia">Pollentia</a>. Talaiotic traditions faded under Roman rule, but hundreds of monuments survive.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1000 BCE – 123 BCE